  "question": {
    "text": "Under the same reaction conditions, initial concentration of $1.386 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{dm}^{-3}$ of a substance becomes half in 40 s and 20 s through first-order and zero-order kinetics, respectively. Ratio $\\left(\\frac{k_1}{k_0}\\right)$ of the rate constants for first order $\\left(k_1\\right)$ and zero $\\operatorname{order}\\left(k_0\\right)$ of the reaction is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "ns5IE",
      "text": "\n$0.5 \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{dm}^3$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "dBs9L",
      "text": "\n$1.0 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{dm}^3$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Xbl3P",
      "text": "\n$1.5 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{dm}^3$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Ud4pb",
      "text": "\n$2.0 \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{dm}^3$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "First order kinetics, $k_1=\\frac{0.693}{t_{1 / 2}}=\\frac{0.693}{40} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$\nZero order kinetics, $k_1=\\frac{C_0}{2 t_{1 / 2}}=\\frac{1.386}{2 \\times 20}$\nHence, $\\frac{k_1}{k_0}=\\frac{0.693}{1.386}=0.5$",
    "image": null
  },
